  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 141 PM MDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nBECOMING WARMER AND WETTER THROUGH WEDNESDAY:\n\nWith broad southwesterly flow in place, moisture advection will\ncontinue to result in increasing cloud cover and isolated \nshowers through Tuesday. Modest warm air advection will also \nallow for more seasonable temperatures to take hold of the \nregion, despite the increasing cloud cover. Gusty outflow winds \ncannot be ruled out near collapsing showers, but organized \nconvection is not anticipated. \n\nWIDESPREAD RAIN TO CLOSE OUT THE WORK WEEK:\n\nAs a strong low pressure system drops southward along the \nCalifornia coast this week, an upper-level jet noses into the \nFour Corners region bringing remnant moisture from Hurricane \nTrudy. Rainfall coverage will pick up on Wednesday as a result, \nwith widespread showers likely Wednesday night through Thursday \nnight before coverage lessens on Friday. A large majority of the\nprecipitation from this system will fall as rain with the \nsurface low and thus the colder airmass staying well north and \nwest of the forecast area keeping snow levels at or above 12K \nfeet. It's early and these details will be refined as the week \nprogresses, but warm rain processes and the track of the low \nhave caused the forecast to trend towards mountain rain. We will\nbe monitoring the potential for flooding/flash flooding \nconcerns later in the week as this system moves over snow- \ncovered and/or saturated soils from last week's wet weather.",
